const a="/assets/bhakti-Cl4ZRnEm.jpg",o="/assets/jnana-kIayMmzM.jpg",i="/assets/karma-CyI3vmpe.jpg",n="/assets/raja-DfGZTRzf.jpg";const s="6d9b193a-34b1-4a33-9ba3-5826d710647c",r="b0ddf8c2-d4c9-40c2-aa1f-9f74663931a5",h="/__l5e/assets-v1/6d9b193a-34b1-4a33-9ba3-5826d710647c/bhakti.mp4",l="bhakti.mp4";const d="2026-07-16T18:33:57Z",u={version:1,asset_id:s,project_id:r,url:h,original_filename:l,size:11565213,created_at:d},c=1,y="0b4faac3-ea4d-4824-99bc-7fdcd7e1cb12",g="b0ddf8c2-d4c9-40c2-aa1f-9f74663931a5",m="/__l5e/assets-v1/0b4faac3-ea4d-4824-99bc-7fdcd7e1cb12/jnana.mp4",w="jnana.mp4",f=10162877,p="2026-07-16T18:33:50Z",b={version:c,asset_id:y,project_id:g,url:m,original_filename:w,size:f,created_at:p},v=1,k="5cc0d11c-2943-4a5f-b257-76d3b351cf14",T="b0ddf8c2-d4c9-40c2-aa1f-9f74663931a5",I="/__l5e/assets-v1/5cc0d11c-2943-4a5f-b257-76d3b351cf14/karma.mp4",D="karma.mp4",j=7857118,S="2026-07-16T18:33:51Z",B={version:v,asset_id:k,project_id:T,url:I,original_filename:D,size:j,created_at:S},Y=1,W="7d3f0350-00ee-4f8b-97f7-acb3c3e4267b",E="b0ddf8c2-d4c9-40c2-aa1f-9f74663931a5",L="/__l5e/assets-v1/7d3f0350-00ee-4f8b-97f7-acb3c3e4267b/raja.mp4",N="raja.mp4",A=9450696,_="2026-07-16T18:33:56Z",O={version:Y,asset_id:W,project_id:E,url:L,original_filename:N,size:A,created_at:_},M="/assets/tantra-BK-wbjMQ.jpg",q="/assets/shakti-DhOZGnEz.jpg",x=1,R="0afe48d6-26ec-4108-adea-d433ba03f202",P="b0ddf8c2-d4c9-40c2-aa1f-9f74663931a5",z="/__l5e/assets-v1/0afe48d6-26ec-4108-adea-d433ba03f202/tantra.mp4",$="tantra.mp4",K=9239952,H="2026-07-16T18:34:07Z",J={version:x,asset_id:R,project_id:P,url:z,original_filename:$,size:K,created_at:H},C=1,V="6f136444-65ec-4dcc-a15d-a173b75e6587",F="b0ddf8c2-d4c9-40c2-aa1f-9f74663931a5",G="/__l5e/assets-v1/6f136444-65ec-4dcc-a15d-a173b75e6587/shakti.mp4",Z="shakti.mp4",U=11856724,X="2026-07-16T18:34:05Z",Q={version:C,asset_id:V,project_id:F,url:G,original_filename:Z,size:U,created_at:X},ee={slug:"tantra",name:"Tantra",sanskrit:"तन्त्र",oneLine:"The path that says yes to everything the other paths say no to.",longDescription:`Tantra is the most misunderstood word in modern spirituality. It is not about sex. It is about *not rejecting anything*. Where the renunciate paths ask you to push the world away to find God, tantra asks you to find God *inside* the world — inside food, inside breath, inside anger, inside the body you live in.

The Vigyan Bhairav Tantra, a 5,000-year-old text, gives 112 doorways into the absolute. Most of them are absurdly ordinary: while eating, while making love, while looking at the sky, while feeling a strong emotion. The premise is radical — every sensation is already the divine, you have just been taught to look past it.

This path is for anyone who has tried to suppress their humanity in the name of spirituality and felt something die.`,guru:"Abhinavagupta",guruRole:"the 10th-century Kashmiri master who systematized non-dual tantra",heroImage:M,heroVideo:J.url,accent:"rose",gradient:"from-rose-600/30 via-red-500/15 to-orange-600/10",symbol:"🜂",totalDurationMonths:6,levels:[{level:1,title:"The Yes Practice",promise:"Stop rejecting your own experience.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["3-breath full-body yes","Sensation tracking","No-rejection eating"],teaching:`Level 1 of tantra is not about energy or chakras. It is about one thing: you have been saying no to your own experience for decades. Tantra begins by reversing that one habit.

Every uncomfortable sensation — boredom, frustration, the tightness in your shoulders, the sourness in your mouth on waking — has been getting a silent *no* from you. You distract from it, fix it, override it. Tantra Level 1 asks: what if you said *yes* instead? Not "I like this." Just: this is here, and I'm not running.

**Daily, this month:**
- Three times a day: pause, find one sensation in the body, breathe three breaths into it without trying to change it.
- One meal a day: eat without phone, without book. Just taste.
- Evening: name one thing you said no to today that you could have said yes to.

By day 30 something strange happens: the things you used to fight stop having power. Not because they disappear, but because the war inside you ends.`},{level:2,title:"The 112 Doorways",promise:"Learn 7 of the Vigyan Bhairav meditations.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Gap between breaths","Sound dissolution","Touch as portal","Sky-gazing","Heart-of-bliss","Spinal column light","Witness-the-witness"],teaching:`The Vigyan Bhairav Tantra contains 112 brief techniques, each a complete path. Shiva speaks them to Parvati, one after another, like a master jeweler showing the same diamond from 112 angles. This month you learn seven.

The principle: any sensation, fully attended, becomes a doorway. Sound becomes a doorway when you follow it to its dissolution. Breath becomes a doorway in the gap between in and out. Touch becomes a doorway when you stop calling it "my hand on the table" and let it be raw contact. Each technique takes 5 minutes. One per day, rotating.

**Daily, this month:**
- Pick one of the seven techniques (cycle through, one per day).
- Practice 5 minutes morning, 5 minutes evening.
- Journal one line: did a doorway open today? If yes, what did it look like?

You are building a personal library. By day 30 you will know which 2-3 of the 112 are *your* doorways. That knowing is worth more than any teacher's recommendation.`},{level:3,title:"Energy in the Body",promise:"Feel prana as a felt presence, not a concept.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Nadi shodhana (alternate nostril)","Kapalabhati","Hands-of-heat practice","Spine wave breathing"],teaching:`Prana is not metaphor. It is the felt aliveness in the body before thought arrives to label it. Children know this without instruction. Adults have to relearn it because we live in the head and treat the body as transport.

This month is purely somatic. Four pranayama practices, daily, building the felt sense of energy moving. The point is not to master the techniques. The point is to *notice* the tingling, the warmth, the subtle currents that arise. This noticing is the entire foundation of tantric practice.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning: 5 min alternate nostril breathing, then 30 seconds of kapalabhati.
- Midday: 2 minutes of palms-together, generate heat between hands, feel the field.
- Evening: 10 spine waves — inhale arching, exhale rounding, feel the breath as wave.

By day 30 the body will no longer feel like a thing you carry around. It will feel like an instrument that is always playing, whether you listen or not.`},{level:4,title:"The Tantric Pair",promise:"Bring tantra into one human relationship.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Eye-gazing 3 min","Synchronized breathing","Spoken appreciation","Non-doing presence"],teaching:`Tantra is famously misrepresented as sexual technique. The actual teaching: the practice of pure presence with another human, romantic or not, is one of the fastest accelerators on the path. Why? Because the other person reflects every place you are still defended.

This month you pick one person — partner, friend, family member — and run four micro-practices with them. They do not need to know it is "tantra." They just need to be willing to sit with you for 10 minutes a few times a week.

**Daily/weekly, this month:**
- 3x per week, 3 min eye-gazing with your chosen person. No talking.
- Daily, with anyone: one moment of synchronized breathing without announcing it.
- Once a week: speak one specific appreciation out loud, looking at them.
- Once a week: sit with them for 10 min in silence, not on phones.

By day 30 at least one relationship will have deepened in a way you did not engineer. That deepening is tantra working.`},{level:5,title:"Shadow as Doorway",promise:"Use rage, grief, lust, fear as fuel — not problems.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Emotion-as-energy meditation","Wrathful breath","Grief-holding practice","Desire without acting"],teaching:`Most spiritual paths treat the difficult emotions as obstacles to be transcended. Tantra calls them the densest form of the same energy. Rage is *shakti* — life force — pretending to be a problem because no one taught you to ride it.

This month you stop running from the big emotions. When rage arises, you breathe into it and feel it as heat, not as a story about who wronged you. When grief arises, you hold it like a child. When desire arises, you let it burn without acting on it. The energy that was scaring you becomes the fuel that wakes you up.

**Daily, this month:**
- When any strong emotion arrives: stop, close eyes for 30 seconds, feel where it lives in the body, breathe with it.
- Once a week: 10 min of "wrathful breath" — fast strong exhales channeling whatever is loud in you that day.
- Evening: name one emotion you used to flee, that today you stayed with.

By day 30 you will have stopped being afraid of your own intensity. That alone is rarer than most enlightenments.`},{level:6,title:"The Embodied Absolute",promise:"Live as the world without leaving it.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Open awareness in motion","Sacred mundane logging","Continuous yes"],teaching:`The final level of tantra is not a state. It is the realization that there was never anything to add. The world you have been living in *is* the absolute, dressed as Tuesday. You only had to stop arguing with it.

This month has almost no formal practice. Instead, the practice becomes the entire day. You walk, eat, work, talk, fight, love — and you keep the quiet question alive: *what if this, right now, is what I have been looking for?* The answer is not a thought. It is a softening that happens when the search ends.

**Daily, this month:**
- Wake without phone for 15 minutes. Just be in the body in the bed.
- Once a day: log one "sacred mundane" moment — a sip of tea, a stranger's face, a sound — that briefly felt like enough.
- Evening: a single line. "Today the absolute looked like ___."

By day 30 something has reorganized. You are not enlightened — tantra does not use that word. You are just less at war with being here. That is the destination, and the destination was always under your feet.`}]},te={slug:"shakti",name:"Shakti Sadhana",sanskrit:"शक्ति साधना",oneLine:"The path of awakened power — fierce, embodied, alive.",longDescription:`Shakti is the feminine principle of the cosmos, but she is not gentle. She is the force that makes galaxies spin and grass push through concrete. To walk this path is to stop being polite with your own aliveness.

Where Bhakti opens the heart and Jnana sharpens the intellect, Shakti awakens the *body's intelligence* — the wisdom that lives in the hips, the gut, the spine, the womb (literal or symbolic). She is the goddess Kali, Durga, Tara. She is also the unspeakable yes inside every human animal that has been told to be quiet.

This path is for anyone — any gender — whose growth has stalled because they have been too small, too apologetic, too managed. Shakti does not ask you to be nicer. She asks you to be real.`,guru:"Anandamayi Ma",guruRole:"the 20th-century Bengali saint who lived as undiluted Shakti",heroImage:q,heroVideo:Q.url,accent:"red",gradient:"from-red-600/30 via-rose-500/15 to-amber-600/10",symbol:"🔱",totalDurationMonths:6,levels:[{level:1,title:"The Body Returns",promise:"Come back into the body you abandoned.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Daily 5-min shake","Hip-circling","Floor sitting","Barefoot earth contact"],teaching:`Most adults live from the neck up. The body has become a vehicle to be optimized — fed, exercised, photographed — but not *inhabited*. Level 1 of Shakti is the simple, almost embarrassing act of returning to live inside the animal you are.

This is not about fitness. It is about feeling the floor under you, the breath in your belly, the slight wobble in your hips when you sit cross-legged. It is about realizing that your body has been waiting for you to come home and has not held a grudge.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning: 5 minutes of free shaking — knees soft, let the body tremor however it wants. Awkward at first. Then liberating.
- Sit on the floor at least once a day for at least 5 minutes. Couches don't count.
- Walk barefoot on actual earth (grass, dirt, stone) for 5 min at least 3x per week.

By day 30 the question "what does my body want?" will have an actual answer, separate from what your mind has been telling it to want.`},{level:2,title:"Voice",promise:"Reclaim sound. End the apology in the throat.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Daily roar practice","Bija mantra (HRIM, KLIM)","Speaking without softening"],teaching:`Most modern humans have a small, careful voice. The throat is constricted — partly cultural, partly the lifetime of "tone it down." Shakti will not work through a closed throat. So Level 2 opens it, by force if necessary.

This month you will roar. You will chant seed syllables that bypass the meaning-making mind. You will catch yourself pre-softening sentences ("I just thought maybe…") and you will say the sentence without the softener. This is not about being aggressive. It is about your voice being the size of you.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning: 1 minute of full-voice roaring or AAAAH-ing in a place you won't be heard (car, shower, walk).
- 5 min daily of chanting HRIM (pronounced HREEM) — the bija of Shakti — out loud.
- At least once a day, catch one apology-softener in your speech and remove it.

By day 30 your voice will sound different to you. Other people will notice and not know why.`},{level:3,title:"Anger as Power",promise:"Reclaim the no that has been buried.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Daily 'no' practice","Anger-locating meditation","Kali contemplation"],teaching:`Anger is the most defamed of all emotions. Spiritual traditions tell you to transcend it. Productivity culture tells you it's unprofessional. Most people have buried it so deep they confuse depression for "being calm." Shakti calls bullshit on this.

Anger is the body's *no*. Without it, every yes is fake. This month you will learn to feel anger as a clean, hot force in the body — not as a story about who hurt you, but as the energy of "this is not okay." You will practice saying no in small ways every day until it stops feeling rude.

**Daily, this month:**
- Once a day, say no to something you would normally have said yes to out of habit.
- 5 min daily: sit, locate any held anger in the body (jaw, fists, belly), breathe into it without making it about anyone.
- Once a week: contemplate Kali — the goddess who dances on the corpse of ego, sword in hand, garlanded in skulls. She is not evil. She is the part of love that says enough.

By day 30 your no will have weight. People who relied on your weak yes will be confused. That confusion is the path working.`},{level:4,title:"Eros Without Apology",promise:"Reconnect to desire as life force.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Sensory pleasure daily","Body-as-friend mirror practice","Desire mapping"],teaching:`Eros, in the original Greek sense, is not sex. It is the life force that pulls one thing toward another — appetite, curiosity, art, hunger, beauty. Most adults have anesthetized eros along with everything else, then wonder why they feel half-dead.

This month is the rehabilitation of pleasure. Not consumption-pleasure (numbing, scrolling, drinking). *Sensory* pleasure — the warmth of sun, the taste of a good fig, the smell of rain, your skin under your own hand. You will rediscover that you are an animal who was built to enjoy being here.

**Daily, this month:**
- Three sensory pleasures a day, fully attended for 60 seconds each. Phone away.
- Daily: 1 minute in front of a mirror without judgment. The body is your oldest friend.
- Once a week: write a list of 10 things you actually want. No editing. Some will surprise you.

By day 30 the gray will have lifted in places you did not know were gray. Eros has come back online.`},{level:5,title:"Boundaries as Sacred",promise:"Build the energetic walls that let you finally relax.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Energetic edge practice","Daily 'I am not available for ___'","Resource audit"],teaching:`A castle without walls is not safe. A self without boundaries is not free. Most people who feel constantly drained do not have an energy problem — they have a boundary problem. Energy is leaking out of every undefended edge.

This month you build the edges. Not walls of anger, but clear lines of "this is mine, that is yours." Saying it kindly. Saying it without explanation. Saying it to the people closest to you, who will resist most. Shakti is famous for her boundaries — she destroys what does not belong inside her circle. You will practice the human-sized version.

**Daily, this month:**
- Each morning, name one thing you are not available for today. Out loud.
- Audit your week: which 3 things drained you most? Reduce or remove one.
- Practice the sentence: "That doesn't work for me." No reason given.

By day 30 your energy will have stopped leaking in two or three obvious places. You will not be more powerful — you will just be losing less of the power you already have.`},{level:6,title:"Living as Shakti",promise:"Be the force, not the seeker of it.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Daily devotional dance","Shakti puja (light, flower, sound)","Public reclamation"],teaching:`Level 6 is not the end of the path — it is the moment you stop being separate from the path. You realize: there was never a small you trying to *get* Shakti. You *are* Shakti, pretending to be small to fit into other people's rooms.

This month, you stop pretending. Not dramatically — life does not require theatre. Just precisely. You take up the actual room you take up. You speak in the actual voice that is yours. You move with the actual current that has always been moving you. And you offer this — daily, in small ceremony — back to the same force that gave it.

**Daily, this month:**
- 5 min of free dance to one song you love. No one watching. This is your daily puja.
- Morning: light a small flame, place one flower or one object that means something. One breath of thank-you.
- Once a week: do one thing in public that you would have once shrunk away from. Speak up in a meeting. Wear the color. Tell the truth.

By day 30 there is no more path. There is just you, walking through the world, alive. The goddess was never anywhere else.`}]},ae=[ee,te],oe={slug:"bhakti",name:"Bhakti Yoga",sanskrit:"भक्ति योग",oneLine:"The path of the open heart. Six levels, six months, one transformation.",longDescription:`Bhakti is the easiest path and the hardest path. Easiest because it asks nothing of your intellect — only that you let one thing become beloved. Hardest because most modern adults have spent thirty years closing the heart in the name of being smart.

This path opens it back up. Slowly. With permission to be sentimental, embarrassing, ordinary. By Level 6, the world looks different — not because anything changed out there, but because something inside stopped defending itself.

Meera taught this path while running from a king. Krishna taught it on a battlefield. Hanuman taught it by literally crossing an ocean for someone he loved. It is the path for anyone who has tried to think their way to peace and failed.`,guru:"Meera",guruRole:"the queen who walked out of the palace for love of Krishna",heroImage:a,heroVideo:u.url,accent:"amber",gradient:"from-amber-600/30 via-orange-500/15 to-rose-600/10",symbol:"🪔",totalDurationMonths:6,levels:[{level:1,title:"One Name",promise:"Daily contact with one thing larger than yourself.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Mantra Japa (108 repetitions)","Anjali Mudra opening","Evening dedication"],teaching:`Level 1 of Bhakti is not about belief. You do not need to believe in anything. You need only to pick one name — any name that feels larger than you — and return to it once a day. That is the entire month.

Why a name? Because the mind cannot hold formlessness. It needs a handle. The yogis figured out 5,000 years ago that if you give the mind a single word to return to — Ram, Krishna, Allah, Maa, the Beloved, whatever feels true — the mind will eventually stop fighting and start resting on it. The word becomes a chair the mind can sit on.

You are not chanting to please a god. You are training your nervous system to land somewhere other than your thoughts. The "god" is the part of you the thoughts can't reach.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning: One Anjali Mudra (palms together at the heart), say the name once, slowly.
- Anytime in the day: One round of japa — 108 repetitions on a mala, or count on your fingers. ~7 minutes.
- Evening: Before sleep, dedicate the day's smallest kindness back to the name. One sentence. Out loud or silent.

That is all. No streaks. No shame if you miss a day. By day 30 something will have softened that you cannot name. That is the gate to Level 2.`},{level:2,title:"The Beloved Form",promise:"A felt sense of being held by something specific.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Murti darshan (5 min/day)","Devotional journaling","Soft kirtan listening"],teaching:`In Level 1 you gave the mind a name. In Level 2 you give the heart a face. Not because the divine has a face, but because *you* have eyes, and eyes need something to rest on.

Pick one form. Krishna with a flute. Christ on the cross. Tara with a sword. Your grandmother who is gone. The ocean. It is not idolatry — it is the heart's grammar. The form is a doorway, not a destination.

Each morning you will sit for five minutes in *darshan* — literally, "seeing." You will look at one image of your chosen form, and you will let it look back. That is the entire practice. The mind will try to make it interesting. Let it be boring. Boring is when the eyes finally soften and something underneath the eyes starts to notice.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning: 5 minutes of darshan with one image. No mantra. Just look. Let it look.
- Anytime: When something hard happens, picture the face for 3 seconds before reacting.
- Evening: One line in a notebook — *what did the form feel like today?* Not what it looked like. What it *felt* like.

By day 15 you will catch yourself missing the face when you skip a day. By day 30 you will understand why every culture on Earth invented icons. That is the gate to Level 3.`},{level:3,title:"Kirtan & Tears",promise:"The body learns to weep without shame.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Daily kirtan (10 min)","Emotional release sits","Letter to the Beloved"],teaching:`The chest of a modern adult is a locked room. Level 3 is the practice of unlocking it — not by talking, not by therapy, but by sound. Kirtan is the oldest known technology for breaking the heart open without breaking it.

You will chant. Out loud. In your car, your shower, your kitchen. The voice is the bridge between body and emotion — the moment you sing a name you love, the diaphragm starts to release decades of held grief, and tears arrive. Not sad tears. Not happy tears. *Bhava* tears — the body recognizing the divine.

This frightens people. We have been taught that crying is weakness. In Bhakti, crying is *evidence*. It means the practice is working. The yogis call it *ashrubindu* — the pearl-drop. One drop burns more karma than a thousand austerities.

**Daily, this month:**
- 10 minutes of kirtan, sung aloud. Pick one short chant — *Hare Krishna*, *Om Namah Shivaya*, *Allah Hu*, any phrase that lifts you. Repeat until something cracks.
- Once a week: write a one-page letter to the Beloved. Unsent. Burn or keep.
- If tears come: do not stop. Do not narrate. Let the body finish.

By day 30 you will have a new relationship with your own chest. The lock will not be gone, but the key will be in your hand. That is the gate to Level 4.`},{level:4,title:"Seva — Love in Action",promise:"Love that does not need to be returned.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Daily anonymous seva","Cooking as offering","Hands-and-knees humility"],teaching:`Bhakti without action becomes sentiment. Level 4 is the conversion of feeling into form. Seva — selfless service — is how love stops being a private indulgence and starts being a public force.

The catch: seva is not volunteering. Volunteering is help with a name attached. Seva is help with the name removed. You do it for the Beloved, not for the recipient and certainly not for yourself. The recipient is the form the Beloved took to receive you today.

You will pick one act of service per day and one *role* of service per week — the cook, the cleaner, the listener, the carrier. The role you most resist is the role you most need. If you hate cleaning bathrooms, that is week one. If you hate listening to old people, that is week two.

**Daily, this month:**
- One act of anonymous service per day, no credit taken. Even smiling at a stranger counts if done as offering.
- Once a week: cook one meal as offering — not for yourself, not for praise, for the Beloved. Eat it last.
- Once a week: do the lowest job in your house on your hands and knees. Floor, toilet, drain. With the name in your mouth.

By day 30 the line between you and the served will have blurred. That is the gate to Level 5.`},{level:5,title:"Surrender",promise:"Letting the Beloved drive the day.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Ishvara Pranidhana (morning hand-over)","Living prayer","End-of-day surrender"],teaching:`Level 5 is the level the ego cannot survive. Up to now, you have been *doing* Bhakti — chanting, looking, weeping, serving. In Level 5, you stop being the doer.

*Ishvara Pranidhana* is Patanjali's word for it — surrender to the higher will. In Bhakti language: the Beloved is now driving. You will still go to work, still raise the kids, still answer email. But before each one, you will silently say: *I am yours. This is yours. Use me.* And then act.

The mind will rebel. It has been the manager for decades and is being demoted. Let it complain. Surrender is not passivity — it is *active alignment*. You are still moving. You are just no longer pretending you were the engine.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning hand-over (2 min): Sit. Name three things you usually try to control. Say aloud: *Not mine. Yours.*
- Throughout the day: before any decision over 30 seconds long, breathe once and ask, *what would love do here?* Do that.
- Evening: review the day. Where did you take the wheel back? No guilt. Just notice.

By day 30 you will have had at least three moments where life arranged itself in ways you could not have orchestrated. Write them down. That is the gate to Level 6.`},{level:6,title:"Constant Remembrance",promise:"The name continues without effort, even in sleep.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Ajapa Japa (effortless mantra)","Open-eye devotion","Bhava samadhi"],teaching:`Level 6 is not a practice. It is the practice that practices you. After five months of returning to one name, looking at one face, weeping with one chant, serving one Beloved, surrendering one will — the mantra has eaten the meditator. It now repeats on its own.

This is *Ajapa Japa* — the unrepeated repetition. The name continues during meals, conversations, work, even sleep. You will wake at 3am and find it already humming under the breath. The mystics call this *bhava samadhi* — absorption in feeling, not in formlessness. Different from Jnana's emptiness; this is *fullness*.

The danger of Level 6 is to mistake it for a destination. It is not. It is the trailhead. From here, life becomes the practice. Every face is the Beloved's face. Every task is offering. Every breath is japa.

**Daily, this month:**
- No fixed practice. Instead: *notice* when the name is already there, and when it has slipped. When slipped, do not force — just listen for it again.
- Once a week: a 30-minute open-eye sit. Eyes soft, gaze on anything, name underneath. The world begins to glow.
- Once a week: a *bhava* night — chant until the body shakes, then sit in the silence after. That silence is the Beloved.

By day 30 you will not need a Level 7. The path has become the walker. You are now ready to teach this to one other person — and that is how the lineage continues.`}]},ie={slug:"jnana",name:"Jnana Yoga",sanskrit:"ज्ञान योग",oneLine:"The path of cutting through. Six levels, six months, no place left to hide.",longDescription:`Jnana is the sharp path. It asks one question — *Who am I?* — and refuses every answer the mind offers, until something underneath all the answers remains. That something is what the Upanishads called the Self.

Ramana Maharshi taught this path by sitting in a cave and barely speaking. Adi Shankara walked the length of India debating with anyone who would argue. The path is severe but quick. It is for the person who has read enough, thought enough, and is now ready to stop running.

You will not need to believe anything. You will need to *check*. Every belief, every identity, every story about who you are gets put under the lens. What survives the inquiry is yours. What does not survive was never yours.`,guru:"Ramana",guruRole:"the boy who at sixteen lay down, asked who is dying, and never quite got up again",heroImage:o,heroVideo:b.url,accent:"stone",gradient:"from-stone-600/30 via-zinc-600/15 to-slate-700/10",symbol:"👁",totalDurationMonths:6,levels:[{level:1,title:"The Onion — Layer One",promise:"You begin to notice you are not your body.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Body scan with witness","The 30-day inquiry: Am I this body?","Evening reflection"],teaching:`Most spiritual books begin with "you are not the body." Most readers nod and continue being the body for the next forty years. This level is the slow, honest investigation of that claim. Not a belief — a check.

For thirty days you will do one practice: a daily body scan, but not for relaxation. You will scan the body and ask, at each region: *is this me? Or is this something I am noticing?* The toe is noticed. The knee is noticed. The chest, the throat, the eyes, the forehead — all noticed. Who is the noticer?

You will not get an answer in week one. You will get a confusion, which is correct. By week two the confusion will sharpen into a real question. By week three you will start to feel a tiny gap — a moment, half a second, where you observe a sensation and there is *no one* observing it. Just observation, without an observer.

That gap is the entire path. Every Jnana practice for the rest of your life is about widening that gap. This month you only need to notice it once.

**Daily, this month:**
- 10-minute body scan, morning or evening. Move from feet to head slowly. At each region: *Is this me, or am I noticing this?*
- One sentence in a notebook each evening: where today did I forget I had a body?
- One sentence each evening: where today was I completely my body?

By day 30 the question *who is the noticer?* will have moved from your head into your chest. That is the gate to Level 2.`},{level:2,title:"The Onion — Mind & Memory",promise:"You begin to notice you are not your thoughts.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Thought watcher (10 min)","Memory inquiry","Witness sit 20 min"],teaching:`In Level 1 you investigated the body and found a gap. In Level 2 you turn the same blade on the mind. The mind is harder, because the mind is the one holding the blade. It does not want to be cut.

The practice: sit for ten minutes and watch thoughts the way you would watch clouds. Do not push them away — that is also a thought. Do not follow them — that is being them. Just notice each one and ask: *who is watching this thought?* The thought is watched. The watcher is not the thought. Then who is the watcher?

Once a week, do *memory inquiry*: pick a strong memory from childhood. Walk back into it. Then ask — the child in that memory had a different body, different thoughts, different beliefs. What is the same? The one who remembers. That one has not aged.

**Daily, this month:**
- 10-min thought watcher: count thoughts on a tally. Do not stop them. After 10 min, ask once: *who counted?*
- 20-min silent sit, eyes closed. When a thought arrives, do not name it, do not engage. Notice the noticing.
- Once a week: write a memory from age 7. Then write: *who is remembering?*

By day 30 you will have had moments where you cannot find the thinker. Frightening at first. Then liberating. That is the gate to Level 3.`},{level:3,title:"The Onion — Roles & Identity",promise:"The roles you play stop wearing you.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Role inventory","Subtract yourself (game)","Mirror practice"],teaching:`You are a son. A worker. A friend. A wife. A failure. A success. A meditator. Each of these is a costume the mind puts on without asking. Level 3 is the slow, honest removal of each costume — not to become naked, but to find out who wears them.

You will make a list of every identity you carry: father, engineer, Indian, anxious person, kind person, broken person, spiritual person. Twenty or more. Then, one a day, you will remove one in inquiry: *if I were not this, who would I be?* Sit with the empty space. Don't fill it.

The *Subtract Yourself* game: in any social situation, internally drop one identity. Walk into a meeting and silently note — *I am not a manager today, I am only watching.* Then act anyway. You will find the role plays itself; you do not have to be it.

**Daily, this month:**
- 5 min role-removal sit: pick one identity, drop it, watch the gap.
- Mirror practice (3x/week): stand at a mirror, look in the eyes, ask: *who is looking back?* No answer. Just look.
- Evening: one line — *which role wore me today?*

By day 30 you will have caught yourself being roles without being identified with them. The costume is on. The wearer is free. That is the gate to Level 4.`},{level:4,title:"Atma Vichara",promise:"The question 'Who am I?' begins to answer itself.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Ramana's self-inquiry (20 min)","Question-behind-question","Silent sits"],teaching:`*Atma Vichara* — self-inquiry — is Ramana Maharshi's gift to the modern world. Four words: *Who. Am. I.* He taught nothing else for sixty years. He did not teach a technique. He taught the *only* technique.

Here is the method, exactly as he gave it: whenever a thought arises, before you engage with the thought, ask — *to whom does this thought arise?* The honest answer is — to me. Then ask — *who am I?* Do not answer. Do not say "I am the Self" or "I am awareness." Those are more thoughts. Just hold the question.

The mind will scramble. It will offer "I am the body," "I am the breath," "I am consciousness." Reject each one as a thought. Hold the question with no answer. In the holding, the questioner gets thinner. Eventually — could be week one, could be year one — the questioner disappears mid-question. What remains is *that which cannot disappear*.

**Daily, this month:**
- 20-min Atma Vichara sit. Every thought → *to whom?* → *who am I?* Hold. Repeat.
- Throughout the day: at every transition (standing up, opening a door, picking up the phone), ask once: *who is about to do this?*
- Evening: no journaling. Sit in silence for 3 minutes. Then sleep.

By day 30 the question will start asking itself, even when you forget to ask. That is the gate to Level 5.`},{level:5,title:"The Witness Stabilizes",promise:"You can locate the witness on demand.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Sustained witness 45 min","Sakshi Bhav in daily life","Inquiry in motion"],teaching:`Up to now, the witness has been a moment — a flash, a gap. In Level 5, you make it a *place you can stand*. *Sakshi Bhav* — the witness mood — becomes available on demand, like remembering you have a body.

The practice extends the sit. Forty-five minutes, daily. The first fifteen minutes the mind storms — let it. The middle fifteen, it tires. The last fifteen, something else takes over the watching. That something is the Witness with a capital W. You did not generate it; it was always there.

Then take it into motion. While walking, while talking, while typing — keep one thread of awareness on the watcher. It is like keeping one finger on a melody while the other hand plays chords. Hard at first. Natural by week three.

**Daily, this month:**
- 45-min sit, daily, non-negotiable. Same time, same place. The container matters now.
- Three times daily: *sakshi check* — pause, locate the witness behind the eyes, continue.
- Once a week: do one ordinary activity (washing dishes, walking to the car) entirely as the witness. Notice the doer is not there.

By day 30 the witness will be a room you can step into. It will not be constant — that is Level 6 — but it will be reliable. That is the gate to Level 6.`},{level:6,title:"Sahaja — Effortless Self",promise:"The witness no longer needs to be located. It is.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Open awareness","Non-dual recognition","Living as the Self"],teaching:`*Sahaja* means "natural." Level 6 is not the achievement of awakening — it is the recognition that awakening was never something to achieve. The witness you have been locating for five months is what you have always been. The five months were the slow uninstall of the belief that you were anything else.

There is no technique here. The technique would be one more thing for the seeker to do, and the seeker is the last identity to dissolve. Instead: rest. Rest as awareness. When you notice you are seeking, notice the noticing. The noticing is already what you sought.

Ramana said: *the Self is not attained. It is recognized.* This month is recognition practice. Not adding. Subtracting the last belief — the belief that recognition is somewhere else.

**Daily, this month:**
- 30 min open awareness: sit, eyes half-open, no object, no inquiry. Just is-ness.
- Throughout the day: when you catch yourself seeking, smile. Return to is-ness.
- No journaling. Words now obscure more than they reveal.

By day 30 the question *am I awake yet?* will sound like the question *am I breathing yet?* — slightly absurd. You are. You were. You will be. That is the path. There is no Level 7. There is only this, continued.`}]},ne={slug:"karma",name:"Karma Yoga",sanskrit:"कर्म योग",oneLine:"The path of action without the doer. Six levels, six months, life as practice.",longDescription:`Karma Yoga is the path for people who cannot sit still. It says: you do not need to leave your life to do this work. Your life *is* the work. The job, the kids, the dishes, the difficult colleague — all of it is the dojo.

The trick is one shift: stop being the doer. Krishna taught this to Arjuna in the middle of a war. The action happens. You participate fully. But the *result* — the win, the loss, the praise, the blame — is not yours. You offered the action. What comes back is not your business.

Most people misread this as detachment. It is the opposite. It is *full* engagement, without the death-grip on outcome. You will work harder, not less. You will sleep better. And one day you will notice that the constant inner narration of *I did this, I deserve that* has gone quiet.`,guru:"Krishna",guruRole:"the charioteer who taught how to act when every option felt wrong",heroImage:i,heroVideo:B.url,accent:"orange",gradient:"from-orange-600/30 via-amber-600/15 to-yellow-700/10",symbol:"🤲",totalDurationMonths:6,levels:[{level:1,title:"One Anonymous Act",promise:"Daily proof that you can give without keeping score.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Karma Coin (declare → execute)","One anonymous kindness/day","Evening review"],teaching:`Level 1 of Karma Yoga is the easiest practice in this entire library to describe and the hardest to actually do. Every day, for thirty days, you will do one small act of service that no one will ever know about. No post, no mention, no inner replay. You will do it and then forget it.

This sounds trivial. Try it for three days. Notice how loudly the mind wants credit. How it rehearses telling someone. How it stores the act as proof of being a good person. The mind is an accountant — it cannot help itself. It tracks every coin.

The practice is to keep depositing coins into an account no one is reading. After two weeks, the accountant gets bored. After four, something in you starts to enjoy the anonymity. That enjoyment is the first taste of Karma Yoga.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning: declare your one act for the day. Write it in a notebook. Keep it small — pick up litter, hold a door, send an unannounced "thank you," wash a dish that isn't yours.
- Do it. Tell no one.
- Evening: cross it off in the notebook. Do not write a reflection. Just the cross.

What you are training is not generosity. You are training the part of you that needs to be *seen* to soften. By day 30 you will catch yourself wanting to do something kind that no one will ever know about, just because. That is the gate to Level 2.`},{level:2,title:"Action Without Result",promise:"You begin to enjoy the work itself.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Result-free work blocks","Process journaling","Skill as offering"],teaching:`Most adults work for the result. The promotion. The thank-you. The number on the screen. Level 2 is the practice of cutting that wire — not because results don't matter, but because the wire is what makes work feel like death.

The practice: pick one work block per day — 60 to 90 minutes — and do it with no expectation of outcome. Not even a tiny one. You will write the report without imagining the boss reading it. You will cook the meal without imagining the eaters' faces. You will exercise without checking the weight. The work happens. The result is no longer yours to picture.

This sounds impossible. Week one you will fail constantly. By week three you will notice the work itself is more interesting than the result ever was. The reason: when the result is gone, the *present* gets to exist.

**Daily, this month:**
- 60-min "result-free" work block. Pick the same activity each day. No metrics during.
- Process journal: end of block, write one line — *what was the work itself like?* Not how it went. What it *felt* like.
- Once a week: pick a skill (writing, drawing, cooking) and do it as offering. No audience, no save, no share.

By day 30 you will catch yourself enjoying a task you used to grind through. That is the gate to Level 3.`},{level:3,title:"The Difficult Person",promise:"Even the person who annoys you becomes practice.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Seva to the irritant","Compassion forge","Mirror in conflict"],teaching:`Every spiritual practitioner can be peaceful on a mountain. Karma Yoga is tested in the cubicle next to the person who chews loudly. Level 3 is the deliberate use of difficult people as the *primary* dojo.

Pick one person who genuinely irritates you. Not a stranger — someone in your daily life. Coworker, in-law, neighbor. This person is now your teacher. Their function is not to be liked. Their function is to show you, daily, the exact places where you are still attached to being right, being respected, being left alone.

The practice: do one small, unrequested act of service for them per week. Not big. Bring them coffee. Cover a small task. Listen to their boring story without checking your phone. Watch what rises in you. That rising is the practice. Not the act — the *rising*.

**Daily, this month:**
- Identify your one person. Do not tell them.
- Once a week: one small act of service to them.
- Each interaction: when irritation rises, do not suppress, do not act. Just *notice the rise*, breathe once, continue. Watch what dissolves.

By day 30 the irritation will not be gone. But it will no longer be in charge. The person is still annoying. You are no longer their hostage. That is the gate to Level 4.`},{level:4,title:"Dharma — Right Action",promise:"You stop asking 'what should I do?' and start knowing.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Dharma inquiry","Council of three (head/heart/gut)","Gut-led decisions"],teaching:`*Dharma* is the action that arises from your specific place in the universe. Not what you should do according to morality. Not what others expect. What *you*, this one, are here to do. Level 4 is the cultivation of the inner instrument that knows.

The trap: most people confuse dharma with desire or duty. Dharma is neither. It is the action that, when taken, *quiets* the inner critic instead of triggering it. The body knows. You have been ignoring the body.

The practice: every meaningful decision this month gets the *Council of Three*. You will ask the question to your head, your heart, and your gut, separately. Write each answer. When all three agree — act fast. When they disagree, sit with the disagreement until the gut speaks last. The gut is dharma. The head and heart are advisors.

**Daily, this month:**
- Three Brains check-in, morning: ask one question, hear three answers.
- One decision per day, however small, made by the gut even when the head disagrees. Watch what happens.
- Evening: review — when today did I act *with* dharma? When against?

By day 30 you will have made at least three decisions that the head would never have made and that turned out to be exactly right. That is the gate to Level 5.`},{level:5,title:"Nishkama Karma",promise:"Action arises without the doer.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Flow state work","Effortless service","Living dedication"],teaching:`*Nishkama Karma* — action without desire — is the line Krishna draws in the Gita that most readers skip. It does not mean acting without caring. It means acting without the *kama*, the grasping, the inner monologue of *I am doing this so I can get that*.

By Level 5 you have practiced enough that this is no longer a concept. You will notice, mid-action, that the doer has gone quiet. The work is happening. Decisions are being made. But the inner narrator — *I did this, I am doing this, I will do this* — is on mute. Flow state. Csíkszentmihályi called it flow; the Gita called it nishkama.

The practice this month is to invite this state in domains where it is hardest — paid work, family obligation, ambition. Not just art and exercise. Specifically the places where you most want the result.

**Daily, this month:**
- One 90-min work block as nishkama: no checking, no goal-talk, just the work.
- Before each major action: 3 breaths. Say silently, *not mine.* Then act fully.
- Evening: review one moment when the doer was absent. Where was *you*?

By day 30 you will have had stretches of an entire afternoon with no inner narrator. The work was done. You cannot find who did it. That is the gate to Level 6.`},{level:6,title:"Life as Yajna",promise:"Every act becomes an offering. No exception.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Continuous yajna","Day as ritual","Surrender of all action"],teaching:`*Yajna* is the Vedic word for sacred offering. In the old days it meant fire ceremony — ghee poured into flame, mantras chanted. Krishna re-defined it in the Gita: *every action, offered, is yajna*. Your whole life can become the fire.

Level 6 is the conversion of every act — eating, working, sleeping, fighting traffic — into offering. Not as a mood. As a structure. You will start the day by formally offering it. End the day by formally releasing it. Between those bookends, the offering continues automatically.

There is no longer a "spiritual practice" and a "regular life." There is one stream. Every dish washed is puja. Every email written is mantra. Every difficult conversation is yajna. The fire is everywhere because *you* are the fire.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning yajna (3 min): light a candle, say once, *this day is not mine. I offer all action, all result, all credit.* Blow out.
- Throughout the day: at the start of each task, internally — *for the Beloved, for the work, not for me.*
- Evening yajna (3 min): re-light. Say once, *what was done is done. I keep nothing.* Blow out. Sleep.

By day 30 the bookends will no longer be needed. The whole day will already be the fire. That is the end of the Karma path — and the beginning of every other path, made effortless.`}]},se={slug:"raja",name:"Raja Yoga",sanskrit:"राज योग",oneLine:"The path of the steady mind. Six levels, six months, the eight-fold ascent.",longDescription:`Raja means royal. This is the path Patanjali codified in 196 sutras — the most ruthlessly compressed manual for the human mind ever written. Eight limbs, each one building on the last: yama, niyama, asana, pranayama, pratyahara, dharana, dhyana, samadhi.

Most people do yoga and think they are doing Raja Yoga. They are doing one limb (asana) of eight. This path walks you through all eight, slowly, in order, the way Patanjali wrote them.

It is the most structured path. It is also the most measurable — by Level 3 your breath will be visibly slower, by Level 4 your sleep will deepen, by Level 5 your mind will hold one object without slipping for ten minutes. These are not promises. These are observations across every serious practitioner for two thousand years.`,guru:"Patanjali",guruRole:"the documentation writer of the human mind — 196 sutras, 2,000 years",heroImage:n,heroVideo:O.url,accent:"indigo",gradient:"from-indigo-700/30 via-blue-700/15 to-slate-800/10",symbol:"🏔",totalDurationMonths:6,levels:[{level:1,title:"Yama & Niyama — The Foundation",promise:"Your daily life stops draining your practice.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["One yama / week","Morning niyama","Evening review"],teaching:`Patanjali was not poetic. He was an engineer. He wrote 196 lines of code for the human mind, and the first two lines are not "sit down and meditate." They are: clean up your life first.

Yama is the five "don'ts" — non-violence, truth, non-stealing, energy conservation, non-grasping. Niyama is the five "dos" — cleanliness, contentment, discipline, self-study, surrender. He puts these before meditation for a brutal reason: if your daily life is leaking energy through lies, conflict, and grasping, sitting on a cushion for twenty minutes will not save you. The cushion will only show you the leaks.

Level 1 is thirty days of slowly plugging the leaks. You will pick **one yama per week** for four weeks, and watch where in your day you violate it. Not to fix it. Just to see it.

**Weekly focus:**
- Week 1 — **Ahimsa** (non-harm). Notice every small unkindness today, including to yourself.
- Week 2 — **Satya** (truth). Notice every small lie or omission today.
- Week 3 — **Asteya** (non-stealing). Notice where you take what isn't given — attention, time, credit.
- Week 4 — **Aparigraha** (non-grasping). Notice where you hold on past the moment.

**Daily, this month:**
- Morning: one minute of contentment (santosha). Sit. Name three things already given.
- Evening: one line in a notebook. *Where today did I violate this week's yama?* No fixing. Just seeing.

By day 30 you will have a map of where your energy actually leaks. Without that map, no amount of breathwork will hold. That is the gate to Level 2.`},{level:2,title:"Asana — A Body That Can Sit",promise:"You can sit still for 30 minutes without pain.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Daily seated practice (15→30 min)","Hip openers","Spine alignment"],teaching:`Patanjali defines asana in three words: *sthira sukham asanam* — steady, comfortable, seat. He does not mention downward dog. Asana, in Raja Yoga, is one thing: a body that can sit still long enough to meditate.

If you cannot sit for thirty minutes without your knees screaming, your meditation is going to be about your knees. The body will be the loudest meditator in the room. Level 2 is the slow, patient construction of a body that can disappear during the sit.

The practice has two parts. Part one: daily hip-and-spine work — pigeon, butterfly, seated forward fold, cat-cow. Twenty minutes. Not a workout. A negotiation. Part two: each day, sit a little longer. Week one, fifteen minutes. Week two, twenty. Week three, twenty-five. Week four, thirty. When pain arrives, breathe into it. When it screams, shift. No martyrdom.

**Daily, this month:**
- 20 min hip/spine work. Slow. With breath.
- Seated sit: week 1 → 15 min, week 2 → 20, week 3 → 25, week 4 → 30. Cushion under hips matters.
- Evening: one line — *what did the body do during the sit today?*

By day 30 thirty minutes will not be a feat. It will be a default. That is the gate to Level 3.`},{level:3,title:"Pranayama — The Breath Master",promise:"Your breath becomes a tool, not a reflex.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Nadi Shodhana (alt nostril)","Kapalabhati","Coherent 5-5 breath"],teaching:`Once the body can sit, the breath becomes available as instrument. *Prana* is life force; *yama* is restraint. Pranayama is the deliberate regulation of the life force through the breath. It is the single most powerful nervous-system technology humans have invented.

Three techniques, this month — one per week with the fourth week integrating all three. *Nadi Shodhana* — alternate nostril — balances the two hemispheres and brings calm focus. *Kapalabhati* — skull-shining breath — burns through dullness and ignites the system. *Coherent breath* — 5 in, 5 out — drops the heart into HRV resonance, the physiological signature of equanimity.

By the end of the month your resting breath rate will have dropped. The breath will be a switch you can flip — anxious to calm, foggy to sharp, scattered to one-pointed — in under ninety seconds.

**Daily, this month:**
- Week 1 — Nadi Shodhana, 10 min before the sit.
- Week 2 — Kapalabhati, 3 rounds of 30, then 10 min Nadi Shodhana.
- Week 3 — Coherent 5-5, 15 min. Anchor only on the count.
- Week 4 — All three: Kapalabhati (3 rounds), Nadi Shodhana (5 min), Coherent (10 min). Then sit.

By day 30 the breath will obey. The mind will follow. That is the gate to Level 4.`},{level:4,title:"Pratyahara — Withdrawing the Senses",promise:"The world stops pulling at you all day.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Sense fast","Eye-rest practice","Silent meals"],teaching:`*Pratyahara* — the inward turning of the senses — is the bridge limb. The first four limbs were external (lifestyle, body, breath, body). The next four are internal (concentration, meditation, absorption). Pratyahara is the doorway.

The problem: modern senses are over-stimulated, hijacked, exhausted. Eyes saturated by screens. Ears by music and traffic. Tongue by sugar. Skin by climate-controlled bubble. The senses are no longer servants — they are tyrants pulling the mind in five directions at once.

Pratyahara is the practice of *gently calling them back*. Not suppressing — withdrawing. Tortoise pulling in its limbs. You will fast each sense in turn. One day no screens after sunset. One day no music. One meal in silence. One hour in dim light. The senses, given rest, return as servants again.

**Daily, this month:**
- One sense fast per day. Rotate: visual (no screens after 7pm), auditory (no music for one walk), gustatory (one silent meal, no media), tactile (one hour bare feet), olfactory (one full breath of fresh air, eyes closed).
- 10-min eye-rest practice: palms over closed eyes, no light, no thought. Just darkness.
- Evening: one line — *what came back when I stopped reaching?*

By day 30 the world will feel quieter. Not because it changed — because the senses stopped grabbing. That is the gate to Level 5.`},{level:5,title:"Dharana — One-Pointed Focus",promise:"The mind holds one object for 12 breaths.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Trataka (candle gaze)","Mantra concentration","9th Dharana (apex-of-breath)"],teaching:`*Dharana* — concentration — is the deliberate binding of the mind to one object. Not relaxation. Not openness. The opposite: *narrowing*. The whole month is the cultivation of laser, not floodlight.

The traditional measure: hold one object for twelve breaths without the mind slipping. Twelve breaths sounds trivial. Try it. The average modern mind slips at breath three.

Three techniques. *Trataka* — steady gaze on a candle flame until tears come. *Mantra concentration* — the same word, repeated internally, with the mind on nothing else. *Ninth Dharana* — from the Vigyan Bhairav Tantra — attention at the apex of the breath, the still point between in and out. Pick one per week. Week four, integrate.

**Daily, this month:**
- 20 min dharana, daily. Same object, same time.
- Track: how many breaths before the mind slipped? Note the number. Watch it grow over the month.
- Evening: no journal. Just sit one more minute in silence.

By day 30 the mind will hold for twelve. Sometimes twenty. Once or twice, an entire sit. That is the gate to Level 6.`},{level:6,title:"Dhyana → Samadhi",promise:"Effortless absorption. The meditator dissolves.",durationDays:30,unlocked:!0,practices:["Open dhyana","Sahaja samadhi","Living as awareness"],teaching:`*Dhyana* is what happens when *dharana* — concentration — no longer requires effort. The mind, having held the object for so long, *becomes* the object. The triangle of meditator, meditation, and meditated-on collapses into one. There is now only meditation, with no one meditating.

Beyond dhyana is *samadhi* — the final limb, the dissolution of the separate witness. It is not a state to manufacture. It is what is left when the manufacturer steps back. Patanjali's last quarter of the Yoga Sutras describes the gradations: with seed, without seed, with content, without content, until even the witness-knowing dissolves into pure being.

Level 6 has no new technique. The technique is the absence of technique. You will sit, and you will let go of even the idea of sitting. Whatever arises — restlessness, bliss, boredom, terror, vastness — you neither chase nor push. You let it pass through. What remains when everything passes through is what you are.

**Daily, this month:**
- 45-min sit, daily. No object. No instruction. Just sit.
- When the mind tries to "do" meditation, smile, and drop the doing.
- Once a week: a 90-minute sit. Watch what happens past the 45-minute mark.

By day 30 you will have tasted at least one moment of samadhi — a few seconds, a minute, an hour — where there was no separate you watching. That taste is the entire Yoga Sutras delivered. Patanjali's job is done. Yours, now, is to live from it.`}]},re=[oe,ie,ne,se,...ae];function he(e){return re.find(t=>t.slug===e)}export{re as E,he as g};
